{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
Del McCoury version
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[Fm]I was standin' by the [Bb]road in Black Jack [Fm]County.
[Fm]Not knowing that the [Eb]Sheriff paid a [C]bounty,
[Fm]For men like me who [Eb]didn't have a [Bb]penny to their [C]name.
[Fm]He locked my legs in [Bb]35 pounds of [Fm]Black Jack [C]County [Fm]Chain.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[Fm]All we had to [Bb]eat was bread and [Fm]water,
[Fm]Everyday we had to [Eb]build his road a [C]mile and a quarter,
[Fm]A black snake whip would [Eb]sting our backs if [Bb]some poor fool [C]complained,
[Fm]But we couldn't fight back wearing [Bb]35 pounds of [Fm]Black Jack [C]County [Fm]Chain.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Solos
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[Fm]Then one night the [Bb]Sheriff lay a [Fm]sleepin',
[Fm]We all gathered [Eb]round him slowly [C]creepin',
[Fm]Heaven help me [Eb]to forget that [Bb]night in the cold cold [C]rain
[Fm]When we beat him to death wearing [Bb]35 pounds of [Fm]Black Jack [C]County [Fm]Chain
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
[Fm]The wounds have all [Bb]healed and I am [Fm]thankful,
[Fm]And there's nothing left but [Eb]scars around my [C]ankle.
[Fm]But best of all no [Eb]man will ever [Bb]be a slave [C]again,
[Fm]To a black snake whip and [Bb]35 pounds of [Fm]Black Jack [C]County [Fm]Chain.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Outro}
[Fm]But best of all no [Eb]man will ever [Bb]be a slave [C]again,
{end_of_verse}

